Upscaling, texture filtering and FXAA in the dungeon.

Baroque clears the screen in the dungeon by drawing a full-screen black
polygon instead of using the VDP1 erase. The renderer only started a new
replayable picture at an erase, so its per-frame list grew to the 8192 cap
and the enhanced path fell back to the native 352x224 picture there. An
opaque, untextured, full-screen rectangle now counts as a clear.

--- a/runner/src/vulkan_renderer.c
+++ b/runner/src/vulkan_renderer.c
@@ -460,6 +460,23 @@ static int create_compute(saturn_vk_renderer *r, const char *name, VkPipelineLay
     return 1;
 }
 
+/* An opaque, untextured, axis-aligned polygon covering at least (0,0)-(w,h)
+ * hides everything drawn before it, exactly like a VDP1 erase. Games that
+ * clear with such a polygon instead of the erase function (Baroque in the
+ * dungeon) must still start a new replayable picture there; otherwise the
+ * history grows to its cap and upscaling silently falls back to native.
+ * Mesh, MSB-on, colour calculation and user clipping can leave pixels
+ * showing through, so they never count. */
+static int vdp1_full_clear(const saturn_vk_vdp1_op *o, int w, int h)
+{
+    if (o->kind != SATURN_VK_VDP1_QUAD || o->textured || (o->pmod & 0x8507u)) return 0;
+    const int32_t *v = o->xy;
+    if (v[1] != v[3] || v[5] != v[7] || v[0] != v[6] || v[2] != v[4]) return 0;
+    int x0 = v[0] < v[2] ? v[0] : v[2], x1 = v[0] < v[2] ? v[2] : v[0];
+    int y0 = v[1] < v[5] ? v[1] : v[5], y1 = v[1] < v[5] ? v[5] : v[1];
+    return x0 <= 0 && y0 <= 0 && x1 >= w && y1 >= h;
+}
+
